+<h1>Is Bangalore property overheating? — Heat index</h1>
+<div class="sub">Updated 2026-06-14 · typical home-loan rate 8.0%</div>
+<p><b>Verdict.</b> Bengaluru is decisively heating up in mid-2026: ~24% YoY price growth and ~33% YoY sales jump put it out of step with a softer pan-India market. Fundamentals are still sound — inventory overhang has actually tightened to ~12 months, housing GNPAs remain low at ~1.3%, and the repo rate is comfortable at 5.25% — but launch mix is now ~68% premium/luxury, the asking-vs-registry gap forced a 6–15% guidance-value hike, and affordability is being stretched by the price surge. Risk skews to a luxury-segment air pocket if rates rise or IT hiring softens, since mid-income supply has thinned.</p>
+<h2>The six indicators</h2>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Indicator</th><th>Value</th><th>Status</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody>
+<tr><td><b>Unsold inventory overhang</b><div class="muted" style="font-size:12px">Bengaluru overhang at ~12 months on ~58,900 unsold units — well below the 18–24 month healthy benchmark. Q1 2026 sales +33% YoY (15,603 units) and supply-sales near parity have tightened the market further.</div></td><td>~12 months, trending lower</td><td><span class="badge b-launched">green</span></td><td><a href="https://therealtytoday.com/news/market-insights/indias-housing-market-shows-strong-structural-stability-in-q1-2026-bengaluru-drives-growth-momentum-mmr-continues-to-anchor-sales/" rel="noopener nofollow">ANAROCK / PropEquity Q1 2026</a></td></tr>
+<tr><td><b>Launch mix skew to luxury</b><div class="muted" style="font-size:12px">High-end and luxury accounted for ~68% of Bengaluru launches in Q1 2026; mid-segment fell to ~31%. Affordable supply has nearly disappeared and &#x27;early fatigue&#x27; is being flagged in the ultra-premium segment.</div></td><td>~68% premium/luxury share of Q1 2026 launches</td><td><span class="badge b-uc">red</span></td><td><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/finance/personal-finance/luxury-homes-drive-bengaluru-realty-but-ultra-premium-shows-early-fatigue-125111800804_1.html" rel="noopener nofollow">PropTiger Real Insight Q1 2026 / Business Standard</a></td></tr>
+<tr><td><b>EMI-to-income (affordability)</b><div class="muted" style="font-size:12px">Knight Frank&#x27;s index shows Bengaluru affordability comfortably under the 50% stress line, but 24% YoY price growth is now outpacing income growth and eroding the cushion. Repo steady at 5.25%; home loan rates 7.65–8.50%.</div></td><td>EMI/income ~26-27% (below 50% threshold but stretched)</td><td><span class="badge b-pre">amber</span></td><td><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/finance/personal-finance/ahmedabad-kolkata-pune-most-affordable-mumbai-falls-below-50-emi-ratio-125122300780_1.html" rel="noopener nofollow">Knight Frank India Affordability Index H1 2026</a></td></tr>
+<tr><td><b>Housing-loan defaults (NPAs)</b><div class="muted" style="font-size:12px">RBI Financial Stability Report (Dec 2025) shows retail housing GNPA stable at ~1.3% with no significant systemic risk. Watch unsecured retail at 1.7% — slippages there could spill over if rates rise. No public locality-level default data.</div></td><td>Retail housing GNPA ~1.3%; system GNPA ~2.1%</td><td><span class="badge b-launched">green</span></td><td><a href="https://www.nextias.com/ca/current-affairs/01-01-2026/rbi-financial-stability-report-2" rel="noopener nofollow">RBI Financial Stability Report Dec 2025</a></td></tr>
+<tr><td><b>Asking vs registered price gap</b><div class="muted" style="font-size:12px">Karnataka hiked guidance values 6–15% in Feb 2026 because the gap to actual transaction prices had widened materially in East and North Bengaluru. Registration fee also doubled (1% → 2%) in Aug 2025 — both signs of an overheated transaction market the state is moving to capture.</div></td><td>Guidance values revised up 6–15% Feb 2026 to close gap</td><td><span class="badge b-pre">amber</span></td><td><a href="https://www.propnewz.com/blog/karnataka-guidance-value-hike-bangalore-impact-2026" rel="noopener nofollow">Kaveri / IGR Karnataka</a></td></tr>
+<tr><td><b>Registration volumes</b><div class="muted" style="font-size:12px">Bengaluru is the only major metro with simultaneous peak sequential sales growth and 24% YoY price appreciation. Registration fee hike and guidance-value revision boost state collections, but velocity at these prices is a classic late-cycle froth signal.</div></td><td>Bengaluru sales +33% YoY in Q1 2026 (15,603 units)</td><td><span class="badge b-uc">red</span></td><td><a href="https://newsable.asianetnews.com/business/bengaluru-leads-indias-housing-boom-with-24-price-surge-in-q1-2026-articleshow-4l0dnn8" rel="noopener nofollow">PropEquity Q1 2026</a></td></tr>
+</tbody></table>
+<p><a class="cta" href="/?heat=1">Open live Heat index on the map →</a></p>
